#!/usr/bin/env python3
#
# gce-gcs-curl --- send a request to the LTM or KCS server via GCS
#
# This program accepts (a subset of) the command-line options of curl,
# but instead of connecting to the LTM or KCS server, it relays the
# request through the gce-xfstests GCS bucket: the request is uploaded
# to the bucket, the gce-gcs-relay daemon running on the server's VM
# hands it to the server, and uploads the server's response to the
# bucket, where this program picks it up.  It is used when gce-xfstests
# is configured with GCE_CURL=gce-gcs-curl, which makes it possible to
# use the LTM and KCS servers when there is no direct network path from
# the machine running gce-xfstests to the server VM's https port (for
# example, because of firewall rules, or because the VM has no external
# IP address).  The price is latency: each request takes a number of
# seconds instead of a fraction of a second.
#
# See the "GCS relay" section of Documentation/gce-xfstests.md for how
# to use this, and the comments at the beginning of
# test-appliance/files/usr/local/lib/gce-gcs-relay for a description of
# the protocol.
#
# The gcloud command is used for all access to GCS, so that it works
# with however the user has set up gcloud's credentials.  Only object
# access on GS_BUCKET is required; the client does not touch Compute
# Engine instance metadata.
#
# The following environment variables, which are set up by gce-xfstests
# (see run_gce_curl in util/get-config), say where the request should
# be sent:
#
#     GS_BUCKET       the GCS bucket used by gce-xfstests
#     GCE_PROJECT     the GCE project
#     GCE_INSTANCE    the name of the VM running the LTM or KCS server
#     GCE_ACCOUNT     (optional) the account which gcloud should use
#
# The following environment variables may be used to tune its behavior:
#
#     GCE_GCS_CURL_TIMEOUT    how many seconds to wait for the response
#                             if -m/--max-time is not given (default: 120)
#     GCE_GCS_CURL_DEBUG      if non-empty, act as if -v had been given
#
# Differences from curl:
#
#   * Only the options listed by "gce-gcs-curl --help" are supported,
#     only https:// URLs are supported, and the port must be 443.
#   * The host name in the URL is used for the Host header and for
#     cookies, but it is not otherwise used to find the server; that is
#     done using GCE_INSTANCE.  Options having to do with setting up the
#     connection (--resolve, --cacert, -k, etc.) are accepted and
#     ignored, since the connection to the server is made by the relay
#     daemon, on the server's own VM.
#   * Fatal errors are always reported on stderr, even if -s is given
#     without -S, since otherwise problems with the relay are very hard
#     to diagnose.
#   * If no response was received, the cookie jar named by -c is left
#     alone instead of being overwritten with an empty one.
#
# The exit status follows curl's conventions: 0 if a response was
# received (whatever its HTTP status, unless -f is given), 2 for usage
# errors, 7 if the server could not be reached, 22 for an HTTP error
# when -f is given, 28 for a timeout, 56 for other relay failures.

